 ## Overview
-Back-face rasterized volume rendering is a GPU-accelerated technique that determines ray entry and exit points for volume traversal by rasterizing the volume's bounding box geometry, avoiding expensive explicit ray-box intersection calculations in the final rendering pass [S1, S2, S3, S4, unofficial]. It differs from [Bounding Box Raymarching](2071-bounding-box-raymarching.md) by explicitly utilizing a two-pass approach where the first pass captures back-face coordinates into a texture, and the second pass uses these coordinates to define ray endpoints for front-to-back compositing [S1, S2, S3, unofficial]. Unlike [Volume Ray Casting with RayData Texture](3077-volume-ray-casting-with-raydata-texture.md), which stores ray data in a dedicated texture, this method often computes ray directions from geometry attributes or camera matrices directly in the fragment shader [S5, unofficial].
+Back-face rasterized volume rendering is a hardware-accelerated technique that determines ray entry and exit points by rasterizing the back faces of the volume's bounding box geometry into a texture, then using those coordinates to drive ray marching in a second pass [S1, S2, S4, unofficial]. It solves the problem of efficiently restricting fragment shader execution to pixels intersecting the volume while avoiding the computational cost of explicit ray-box intersection calculations in the fragment shader [S2, S3, unofficial]. Unlike [Bounding Box Raymarching](2071-bounding-box-raymarching.md), which may compute intersections explicitly or use different culling strategies, this method explicitly decouples boundary detection into a dedicated first pass that stores back-face coordinates, ensuring correct handling of zoomed-in views where front faces might project behind the camera [S2, S3, unofficial].
 
